## Why This Question Is Asked
This evaluates practical controls for vendor-integrated cloud data access and cross-service trust boundaries.

## Key Concepts
- PKCE and short-lived tokens prevent token replay and limit exposure windows
- Token binding and mTLS establish bidirectional trust between services
- Dynamic credential rotation minimizes the blast radius of leaked secrets
- Policy-as-code IAM checks enable automated, repeatable security enforcement
- Incident response playbooks ensure rapid containment and recovery

## Code Example
```javascript
const verifyPKCE = (codeChallenge, codeVerifier, method = 'S256') => {
  if (method === 'S256') {
    const hash = crypto.createHash('sha256')
      .update(codeVerifier)
      .digest('base64url');
    return hash === codeChallenge;
  }
  return codeVerifier === codeChallenge;
};

const validateTokenBinding = (token, clientCert, deviceFingerprint) => {
  return token.binding.certThumbprint === clientCert.thumbprint
    && token.binding.deviceId === deviceFingerprint.id;
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests basic data protection practices for logging in a multi-tenant analytics platform, focusing on practical steps to prevent credential leakage without overhauling the system.

## Key Concepts

- Log redaction and sanitization
- Structured logging and ingestion pipelines
- Per-tenant data masking
- Token rotation policies
- Incident response basics for credential leakage

## Code Example

```javascript
function redactLog(event){
  try {
    const s = JSON.stringify(event);
    return s.replace(/token=[^\s&]+/gi, 'token=REDACTED');
  } catch (e) {
    return '{}';
  }
}
```

